 have:\n- One pung: [W1,W1,W1]\n- One chow: [B2,B3,B4]\n- In hand: W6,W7,W7,W8,W9,T4,B3,B4\n\nIf you convert the W1 pung into part of chows? No \u2014 pung is fixed.\n\nUnless you discard W1s \u2014 but you can\u2019t.\n\nYou\u2019re stuck with one pung.\n\nSo maximum chows: one now, plus possibly three more? But pung blocks All Chows.\n\nSo **All Chows** is impossible.\n\nBack to scoring.\n\nPossible patterns:\n- **Mixed Triple Chow** (8 points): Three chows of same numbers in different suits. Unlikely \u2014 you only have Wans and one T4.\n- **Mixed Shifted Pungs** (8 points): Three pungs in different suits, consecutive numbers. You have only one pung (W1).\n- **Triple Pung** (16 points): Three pungs of same number \u2014 no.\n- **Pure Straight** (16 points): Needs 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in same suit. You have W6,W7,W8,W9,W7 \u2014 missing W1-W3 and W4-W5. W4 is gone (0 left), so impossible.\n- **Outside Hand** (4 points): Every meld and pair contains terminals or honors. Your [B2,B3,B4] is all simples \u2014 B2,B3,B4 are 2,3,4 \u2014 not terminals. So invalid.\n- **All Simples** (2 points): No terminals or honors. W1 pung is terminal \u2014 so invalid.\n\nSo your current hand has:\n- [W1,W1,W1]: pung of terminal \u2014 worth **Pung of Terminals or Honors** (1 point)\n- Potential for two chows in Wans: W6-W7-W8 and W7-W8-W9 \u2192 **Short Straight** (2 points)\n- Possibly **All Pungs**? No.\n- **Melded Hand** (6 points)? All melds exposed \u2014 yes, you have Peng W1 and Chi B3 \u2014 both exposed. And if you win by claiming a tile, and pair is completed by opponent, then yes. But you don\u2019t have a pair yet.\n\nWait \u2014 you need a pair.\n\nCurrently, your hand has 8 tiles: after discarding one, you\u2019ll have 7, then draw to 8, etc.\n\nBut ultimately, you need four melds and a pair.\n\nCurrent state:\n- Melds: [W1,W1,W1] (pung), [B2,B3,B4] (chow)\n- Hand: W6, W7, W7, W8, W9, T4, B3, B4 \u2014 8 tiles\n\nAfter discard: 7 tiles \u2192 need to form two more melds and a pair.\n\nBut you already have two melds, so need two more melds (or one meld and a pair via wait).\n\nBut better to aim for **Chicken Hand (8 points)** as baseline.\n\nNow, look at discards:\n\n- **Play T4**: removes a Dots tile, keeps all Wans together. T4 is relatively safe (T4:2 left unshown), but not particularly useful. This improves cohesion of hand \u2014 all remaining non-meld tiles are Wans except T4. So best to discard T4 to focus on Wans-based chows.\n\n- **Play B3 or B4**: risky, since you may be helping others who are collecting Bamboo. B3: only 1 left unshown \u2014 likely someone is watching it. Similarly B4: 2 left. But you have them in hand \u2014 but since you already have a Chow using them, having extras is okay, but not necessary.\n\n- **Play W6, W7, W8, W9**: all are key to forming chows. W7 has two copies \u2014 safest to keep. W6, W8, W9 are all critical.\n\nBest discard: **T4** \u2014 it\u2019s the only non-Wan tile in your hand that isn\u2019t part of a future plan.\n\nBy playing T4, you commit to building two chows in Wans:\n- W6-W7-W8\n- W7-W8-W9\n\nWhich gives you **Short Straight** (2 points) + possibly **Pure Double Chow** if you count W7-W8-W9 twice? No.\n\nBut **Short Straight** is solid.\n\nAlso, you have a **Pung of Terminals** (W1) \u2192 1 point.\n\nOther possibilities: **Concealed Hand**? No \u2014 you have exposed melds.\n\n**Self Drawn** (1 point) if you draw winning tile.\n\nBut currently, you need to improve your hand structure.\n\nAnother idea: **Upper Tiles** (24 points): only 7,8,9 tiles.\n\nCheck: your Wans: W6,W7,W7,W8,W9 \u2014 W6 is 6, not allowed. So if you can eliminate W6, and only keep W7,W8,W9, then you can go for **Upper Tiles**.\n\nYes! **Upper Tiles** requires all tiles to be 7,8,9 of suited tiles.\n\nYour melds:\n- [W1,W1,W1]: W1 is 1 \u2014 not allowed. So invalid.\n\nBecause of W1 pung, you **cannot** qualify for **Upper Tiles**, **Lower Tiles**, **Middle Tiles**, **All Simples**, etc. \u2014 W1 is terminal.\n\nSo your hand is anchored by a terminal pung, limiting options.\n\nThus, best you can do is **Chicken Hand** (8 points) with combinations like:\n- Pung of Terminals or Honors (1)\n- Short Straight (2)\n- Possibly Mixed Double Chow? No.\n- Or just rely on Chicken Hand base.\n\nBut **Chicken Hand** is worth 8 points by itself \u2014 and includes hands that would score 0 otherwise but are valid wins.\n\nBut you might exceed that.\n\nHowever, with W1 pung and mixed meld, likely around 8\u201312 points max.\n\nNow, among valid actions, safest and most logical discard is **T4**, as it\u2019s the only tile not in the Wans suit and not contributing to immediate shape.\n\nOther discards like W6 would hurt your chance to form W6-W7-W8.\n\nSimilarly, W9 is needed for W7-W8-W9.\n\nW7\u00d72 is good \u2014 can serve as eye or part of chow.\n\nB3 and B4 are redundant since already in meld.\n\nBut B3: only 1 left \u2014 maybe dangerous to hold? But not critical.\n\nBest move: **Play T4**\n\nReason: T4 is the only tile in a third suit (Dots) and does not contribute to forming cohesive melds in Wans. Discarding it streamlines your hand toward completing two consecutive chows in Wans (W6-W7-W8 and W7-W8-W9), potentially achieving the **Short Straight** (2 points) pattern. Combined with the **Pung of Terminals or Honors** (1 point) from W1, and possibly other minor patterns upon completion, this keeps you on track for at least a Chicken Hand (8 points). All other discards risk breaking potential chows or retaining less useful tiles.\n\nAnswer: Play T4"}
